WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.445
Good evening, everyone.

00:00:08.445 --> 00:00:16.500
It is my pleasure to invite you all to the postdoc discussion and conversation session

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:22.605
in which we heard from Professor Nisheeth Vishnoi on Friday.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.840
As you all may remember,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.350
Professor Vishnoi who is the Bartlett Giamatti Professor of

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.690
Computer Science and Co-Founder of

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:34.379
the Computation and Society Initiative at Yale University,

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:41.475
does foundational research on computation and as we saw

00:00:41.475 --> 00:00:48.980
from the examples he gave us in his talk, his expertise spans multiple disciplines

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:56.980
and a range of problems that deal with the issue of bias in algorithms.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.780
Today, it is a real treat for us to be able to witness

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.570
a conversation between two stalwarts in

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.430
the field, and our conversant is Professor Aleksander Madry,

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:13.310
who is the Cadence Design Systems Professor of

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:18.139
Computing at the MIT Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Department,

00:01:18.139 --> 00:01:20.570
as well as he's a member of

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:24.530
the Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Lab, CSAIL at MIT.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.370
He received his Ph.D. from MIT in 2011.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.615
Prior to getting, couldn't stray far from MIT.

00:01:32.615 --> 00:01:35.060
Prior to that, he spent a year as

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.530
a postdoctoral researcher at Microsoft Research New England,

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.565
which incidentally is like a stone's throw away from MIT.

00:01:43.565 --> 00:01:48.665
But he did earn his chops by going further afar.

00:01:48.665 --> 00:01:53.555
He was on the faculty at EPFL until early 2015.

00:01:53.555 --> 00:01:55.940
He currently serves as the director of

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:02.210
the MIT Center for Deployable Machine Learning and is the faculty lead of the CSAIL-MSR

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.345
Trustworthy and Robust AI Collaboration.

00:02:05.345 --> 00:02:07.985
His research spans machine learning,

00:02:07.985 --> 00:02:11.090
optimization and algorithmic graph theory.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.145
He has a strong interest in building on the existing machine learning techniques to forge

00:02:16.145 --> 00:02:19.730
a decision-making tool kit that is reliable and well understood

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.915
enough to be safely and responsibly deployed in the real world.

00:02:23.915 --> 00:02:28.205
You can see how these two interlocutors are

00:02:28.205 --> 00:02:33.230
going to engage and teach us a lot about some of

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:40.190
the challenges that Professor Vishnoi brought to the fore for bias in

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:47.180
algorithms and since we are going to encounter them in our daily lives,

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.965
whether we like it or not, that for the future,

00:02:50.965 --> 00:02:54.080
issues of understanding bias,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.435
how algorithms reveal our biases and how, in turn,

00:02:59.435 --> 00:03:01.850
not only do they expose us,

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:08.660
but how we can actually learn and mitigate our own biases,

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.835
are the questions that we are hoping to learn from both of you.

00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:21.475
Aleksander, I invite you to take it away and get started with the discussion. Welcome.

00:03:21.475 --> 00:03:23.045
Welcome, everyone.

00:03:23.045 --> 00:03:24.604
Thank you for the invitation.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:29.060
It's really a treat to be here and treat for many reasons.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.290
In particular, Nisheeth is someone that is a close friend,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:38.675
and there is a lot of parallel between mine and his trajectory.

00:03:38.675 --> 00:03:42.890
In particular, he also was a faculty at EPFL.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.217
We almost overlapped.

00:03:44.217 --> 00:03:49.580
I guess we technically overlapped, and that's kind of intensive institutions.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.250
But I think more importantly, intellectually,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.755
there is a lot of parallel between how

00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:59.645
our research trajectories evolved and what questions we were drawn to.

00:03:59.645 --> 00:04:02.015
In particular, both Nisheeth and I,

00:04:02.015 --> 00:04:06.930
our roots are in theory of computer science, in optimization algorithms.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.805
But again, it was interesting to see that even though we did not interact that much,

00:04:10.805 --> 00:04:16.715
our interests in some ways aligned on similar topics, so that says something.

00:04:16.715 --> 00:04:21.550
To be honest, after watching Nisheeth's talk,

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.145
I almost tempted not to have any slides.

00:04:24.145 --> 00:04:27.760
I thought that he did a really great job of providing

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:31.555
a backdrop for a discussion and highlighting very important topics.

00:04:31.555 --> 00:04:33.040
But then since his talk,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.530
he called me out a couple of times that I am

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.380
supposed to say something, I will, and hopefully,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.670
this will provide a little bit of

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:45.445
a different angle at the questions that I will also articulate towards the end,

00:04:45.445 --> 00:04:48.760
and so I hope that after this highlighting,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.290
we can have a very important conversation.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.465
One more thing I want to say is that,

00:04:54.465 --> 00:04:57.015
beyond just me engaging with Nisheeth,

00:04:57.015 --> 00:04:59.740
I want to view it as a little bit even on

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:04.160
a broader sphere in a sense that I really think that these questions,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.860
and that's what Nisheeth beautifully embodies is

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.205
that they really go beyond computing or computer science.

00:05:10.205 --> 00:05:13.640
I think these are the questions that touch on all of science and all of

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.120
our understanding of society and different facets of society.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:25.260
A place like Yale is definitely a great conversation partner for this discussion.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:27.450
I guess Nisheeth can channel much of

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.510
this wisdom there, but I'm looking forward to engaging.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.740
Again, let me get started.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:41.180
Essentially, as I said, similarly to Nisheeth, until maybe 2016,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.455
I was happily doing just theory and that was the only thing I

00:05:45.455 --> 00:05:49.920
cared about, but then some interesting things happened.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.400
We all know that machine learning,

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.710
after some advancement, particularly in computer vision, became the thing.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.170
It became the thing that got everyone's attention

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.770
and started to be actually deployed and put

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:11.000
into just about anything, and I must say that as these things unfolded,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.965
I was somewhat unfazed,

00:06:12.965 --> 00:06:16.340
like not the first hype happening,

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:18.020
not the last one either.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:19.385
It was nice to see,

00:06:19.385 --> 00:06:20.720
it was important to explore,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.065
but I was not personally moved.

00:06:24.065 --> 00:06:26.030
Things changed me, though.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.945
When I started to ask myself the question,

00:06:29.945 --> 00:06:31.760
there was some talk that I was attending,

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.145
just out of curiosity, and the question that I would say, are we there yet?

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:37.835
In some ways, yes,

00:06:37.835 --> 00:06:39.320
we have these advances,

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:41.975
the things that seem to be science fiction just like

00:06:41.975 --> 00:06:45.005
10 years ago, now seem to be easy to do,

00:06:45.005 --> 00:06:48.330
like you touch this, you touch translation, it works.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.650
You touch this, it works,

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.150
so are we done?

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.760
Did we find this silver bullet?

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.560
Essentially, all that will happen now for the next decade or so,

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.410
we will just figure out how to polish things,

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.815
how to scale up things but essentially we are already there.

00:07:03.815 --> 00:07:06.095
That got me thinking about, okay,

00:07:06.095 --> 00:07:10.920
so there are clearly some cool demos happening there, but what does actually would it

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:16.750
take to think about machine learning deployed in a responsible way?

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.535
Of course, to even have a premise

00:07:19.535 --> 00:07:23.075
of a reason to deploy machine learning in the first place,

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:24.300
you need to have performance.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:30.855
There has to be some value that the systems can deliver, and I guess this was done.

00:07:30.855 --> 00:07:32.310
This was provided.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:33.890
There is some value in using

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:37.580
the systems but then you especially being a theorist and thinking of

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:42.920
optimization and different aspects of much of theories about understanding

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:46.160
the phenomena at play deeply and

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.405
understanding all the ways in which none of these ways in which something can fail.

00:07:50.405 --> 00:07:52.429
You start thinking about robustness,

00:07:52.429 --> 00:07:57.800
start thinking about the question of performance on data that is unvetted or untrusted.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.210
You think about reliability,

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:03.140
about trying to know every system fails.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.750
But what is important is that whenever it fails,

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:10.020
whenever it moves away from the conditions that it was designed to work for,

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:11.730
it should fail in a graceful way.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.030
The question is like that's what I call reliability,

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:18.630
like to what extent these machine learning systems exhibit this behavior?

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.370
Then, there is also an additional question of like I call interpretability.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:27.000
There are many other ways, explainability and similar.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.340
But the point was that there could be some desire and

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.080
need for us to be able to inspect the given model to

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.970
provide some quality assurance or think about regulating behavior or essentially

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.250
trying to provide a bit more of

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:46.895
a nuanced understanding of what the model is doing and why.

00:08:46.895 --> 00:08:49.040
When you start asking about these facets,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:50.315
you realize, you ask again,

00:08:50.315 --> 00:08:51.605
do we have that already?

00:08:51.605 --> 00:08:53.630
In particular, much of my work was

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.870
focused on the questions of robustness and reliability,

00:08:55.870 --> 00:09:01.310
the answer becomes quite clear and very obviously, not at all.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:08.750
In particular, much of my work was focused on this prevalent brittleness of

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.720
our current machine learning tools, so one

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:16.340
of the most classical ways of demonstrating this

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:17.915
are something called the [inaudible] little examples.

00:09:17.915 --> 00:09:20.285
Here's an example of an [inaudible] additional example.

00:09:20.285 --> 00:09:23.420
We have this beautiful picture of a pig

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:28.850
which state-of-the-art classifier recognizes as a pig with 91 percent confidence,

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.775
so everything exactly as advertised.

00:09:31.775 --> 00:09:34.865
But now, the funny things start to happen

00:09:34.865 --> 00:09:38.360
if I start to add a little bit of noise to it.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:39.680
This is not a random noise.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.025
This is a noise that is created in a specific way, I will not talk about how.

00:09:43.025 --> 00:09:45.215
But the point is, this is a very tiny noise,

00:09:45.215 --> 00:09:47.870
like the image you get after adding this noise is essentially

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:51.665
indistinguishable to you as a human from the one on the left.

00:09:51.665 --> 00:09:55.145
Essentially, nothing should change except for this model.

00:09:55.145 --> 00:09:58.100
Suddenly, this model is convinced that what we have

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:01.400
here is an airliner with very high confidence,

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.010
and this is the desired thing.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:07.429
This machine learning is able to make pigs fly,

00:10:07.429 --> 00:10:10.265
that's clearly an achievement of strength.

00:10:10.265 --> 00:10:14.300
But of course, this is clearly not what we would intend to happen.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:18.050
Then it goes deeper. Then it's realized this is not just

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:21.575
about me playing with pixels in an image and getting those results.

00:10:21.575 --> 00:10:26.765
There was a bunch of MIT undergrads who created a turtle, that actually

00:10:26.765 --> 00:10:33.365
is classified as a rifle from most angles and views.

00:10:33.365 --> 00:10:36.590
Previously, this was more about

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:42.095
adversarial design in which we tried to engineer for these things to happen.

00:10:42.095 --> 00:10:44.600
But actually, if you look around at the deployment

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.480
of different machine learning systems like in Tesla's,

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.030
you see that there are plenty of examples where

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:53.420
these things just tend to relatively regularly fail.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:56.135
Here, just what happened is this was on a highway,

00:10:56.135 --> 00:10:57.230
there was an overturned truck

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:01.700
and somehow Tesla's autopilot system did not realize that there

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.880
is anything in its way and it just tried to drive.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:08.795
No one got hurt, everything ended up well, but clearly,

00:11:08.795 --> 00:11:11.135
this was a glitch we did not expect.

00:11:11.135 --> 00:11:13.730
Now, I could spend hours to tell you

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:16.370
a little bit more about different facets of this brittleness.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:19.790
But that's not fascinated me, and I think it

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.810
is not something that would be really that important for the discussion.

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:26.900
I think the question that is important to ask ourselves is,

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:28.715
what is the root of this brittleness?

00:11:28.715 --> 00:11:33.275
Why are these machine learning systems so brittle?

00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:36.665
Essentially, long story short is that

00:11:36.665 --> 00:11:39.770
one realization is that once you start looking deeply into that,

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:42.905
you realize that it's about data in large part.

00:11:42.905 --> 00:11:45.215
Like data matters a lot.

00:11:45.215 --> 00:11:48.800
In some ways, especially when we think about machine learning,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.480
we tend to focus on the models.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.610
Because this is what draws our attention.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.530
There is the new transformer model,

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.330
new ResNet model,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.705
and it has that many parameters, and this seems to be the magic.

00:11:59.705 --> 00:12:02.330
But really as much as there is some magic in that,

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:09.770
the huge part of what drives the performance of the systems is data.

00:12:09.770 --> 00:12:12.860
The data that we actually make these models ingest

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.740
and essentially that end up informing decisions.

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:20.555
In particular, this is almost obvious to anyone who thinks about data is that

00:12:20.555 --> 00:12:24.695
essentially if you think about what our machine learning currently does,

00:12:24.695 --> 00:12:28.730
all it does really is an excellent correlation extractor.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:33.995
The way the "prediction" is happening is that during training,

00:12:33.995 --> 00:12:36.740
the model figures out different patterns in

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:40.385
data that correlate well with one label versus the other,

00:12:40.385 --> 00:12:44.000
and then the way the prediction happens on new input is just

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.570
like you look at the patterns present in this image,

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:51.620
and you use this correlation data to make your prediction.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:55.850
On the one hand, this is a very successful approach.

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.925
It does some remarkable things,

00:12:58.925 --> 00:13:04.190
but I actually view it also as a huge problem of the current systems.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.960
Again, it might not be obvious yet,

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:11.630
but many of the things that Nisheeth talked about on Friday actually stem from that.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.990
Of course, the problem is the spurious correlations at the high level.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.240
That causation is not correlation.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:18.755
We all know that by now,

00:13:18.755 --> 00:13:21.680
and the problem is that,

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.260
there are correlations that are not causal,

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:25.865
but essentially are spurious.

00:13:25.865 --> 00:13:30.155
But now, the question is why does it matter so much and why this is such a problem?

00:13:30.155 --> 00:13:32.615
The problem I would say is two-fold.

00:13:32.615 --> 00:13:37.700
First of all, if you know that all my model is doing extracting correlations,

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:41.150
then, well, I can essentially plan these correlations.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:42.590
If I'm adversary in particular,

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:47.060
I can plan these correlations into my data.

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:49.850
Here, let's say I have dogs versus cats training set.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:54.950
If I make the top-left pixel encode whether this is a dog or cat,

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:56.855
what will happen is the model trained on that

00:13:56.855 --> 00:14:00.695
will never even bother to learn how a cat or a dog looks like.

00:14:00.695 --> 00:14:06.005
It will try to read the color of the pixel, that's all it will do.

00:14:06.005 --> 00:14:10.463
This hijacking of the decision process of this model is actually very important.

00:14:10.463 --> 00:14:13.954
In particular in the context of security,

00:14:13.954 --> 00:14:18.215
there are some very interesting and amusing ways of exploiting that.

00:14:18.215 --> 00:14:21.665
This is being the correlated extractors can be

00:14:21.665 --> 00:14:25.070
easily exploited in this way wherever you can play with data maliciously.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:28.010
But I think the more important point particularly for today

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.920
is that these correlations already exist in our data right now.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:35.615
Just one example, even in the data set like ImageNet,

00:14:35.615 --> 00:14:39.170
it turns out that some of the cat versus dog images like

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:42.500
you realize that there are bow ties put on both animals.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:46.220
But it tends to be for some reason cats more often than dogs.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.290
Now, that's a funny fact,

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:51.020
but what happens after that is that

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:53.690
what model learns from such data is

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:57.709
that presence of a bow tie actually is an indication

00:14:57.709 --> 00:15:04.535
that this image is more likely to be a cat image versus a dog image,

00:15:04.535 --> 00:15:09.665
which clearly is not too sound of a conclusion, even if supported by data.

00:15:09.665 --> 00:15:11.780
In fact, this goes even deeper.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:14.495
It turns out that the way we create datasets

00:15:14.495 --> 00:15:17.930
gives rise to these spurious correlations and biases.

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.885
My favorite one is just the question that we might ask.

00:15:20.885 --> 00:15:23.630
ImageNet is one of the big datasets

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:26.630
that much of research and development of ML is based on.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:30.185
You can ask yourself what does a notion of a fish mean

00:15:30.185 --> 00:15:31.910
according to image intake?

00:15:31.910 --> 00:15:36.035
The important background here is that ImageNet is sourced from social media,

00:15:36.035 --> 00:15:37.745
from a website called Flickr.

00:15:37.745 --> 00:15:40.130
You need to start asking yourself,

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:41.570
if I never knew,

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:43.325
like I didn't have a concept of a fish,

00:15:43.325 --> 00:15:44.690
I've never had seen fish.

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:46.790
No one explained to me what a fish is, and I just wanted to

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:51.050
learn it from this data from social media what fish is.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:56.420
Well, it would be kind of an interesting concept of fish

00:15:56.420 --> 00:16:00.365
because most of the pictures of a fish on social media look like that.

00:16:00.365 --> 00:16:03.950
Because people shared on social media only because they want to

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:08.195
show the fish they saw or maybe the fish they ate.

00:16:08.195 --> 00:16:11.600
There are very few photos of someone actually diving in and

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:15.035
trying to take a photo of a fish in its natural environment.

00:16:15.035 --> 00:16:19.070
But now that's natural to us when you think about it but now think

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:23.900
what happens if we give this data to correlation extractor?

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:28.820
Well, what can happen is that the model will learn that essentially having a human hold

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:33.575
something like that in the picture makes it into a fish image.

00:16:33.575 --> 00:16:38.075
Similarly, it learns that the natural environment for a crab is a plate,

00:16:38.075 --> 00:16:41.705
because that's where it sees most of the crabs.

00:16:41.705 --> 00:16:45.320
These are some ways in which this system has learned

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:49.340
biases that we would not think as valid, but well, they are supported by date.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.425
Of course, here we are talking about

00:16:51.425 --> 00:16:57.740
just recognizing cats versus dogs or fish, we might say, who cares.

00:16:57.740 --> 00:17:02.930
But then, these things do matter when we deploy in the real world,

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.900
and in particular the use of machine learning in

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:09.500
the context of medical context is right for examples in

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:13.220
which we have predictive patterns in

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:18.095
the quotes that actually are not what we would like the model to learn.

00:17:18.095 --> 00:17:24.470
This is roughly just a quick run-through of some of the issues that in particular,

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:25.760
I think about a lot,

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:27.560
and again in a moment, I will connect it to

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:30.425
the issue of that Nisheeth thinks about as well.

00:17:30.425 --> 00:17:34.970
Because I think the way I like to understand what's really

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:40.220
going on here is that there is this what I call Human-ML misalignment,

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:44.195
this emergent realization that success at a task,

00:17:44.195 --> 00:17:45.980
the way we phrase it in

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:48.200
a mathematically precise way does

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.810
not mean that the model is doing what you intended it to do,

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:53.735
that it learns the desired concepts that we want.

00:17:53.735 --> 00:17:56.420
Just in the context of vision, if I want to

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:59.195
train my model to recognize dogs by just saying,

00:17:59.195 --> 00:18:02.150
you need to recognize dogs, [inaudible] you need to get the label right,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:06.305
well, we have this expectation that the only way to do it

00:18:06.305 --> 00:18:08.090
is the way the human does,

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:15.185
to learn that there is a different structure of the fur,

00:18:15.185 --> 00:18:18.560
you need to realize what the snout is and so on.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:23.045
That's the only way to perform this task very well.

00:18:23.045 --> 00:18:29.600
But the reality is that it's not true, that ML systems learn very different ways of

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.360
solving the tests that completely do not

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:36.695
involve learning any of the concepts we assume they are learning.

00:18:36.695 --> 00:18:38.690
There is this misalignment with this technology,

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:41.855
it's really using patterns that we as humans

00:18:41.855 --> 00:18:43.460
not only would not use,

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:46.680
but you might not even be able to comprehend.

00:18:47.130 --> 00:18:50.440
Where does it take us now?

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.410
Well, it takes us to first realization that if you

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:56.275
think about essentially all the questions,

00:18:56.275 --> 00:18:58.300
all the problems that we are grappling with,

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:01.015
in the context of ML including the questions of bias,

00:19:01.015 --> 00:19:07.660
they really boil down to this human-ML misalignment, saying we have certain set of

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:09.460
beliefs and then we have

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:15.070
some mathematically precise definitions, and we're trying to equal.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:17.860
The worst things happen if you assume they are the same.

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:22.630
In particular, a lot of work that Nisheeth is doing is essentially trying to

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:26.185
exactly make these mathematically precise definitions

00:19:26.185 --> 00:19:27.865
of a particular what fairness means,

00:19:27.865 --> 00:19:34.345
to meet what is our notion or what will be our desired notion of fairness?

00:19:34.345 --> 00:19:38.440
Again, we will get back to this in our discussion, but then, of

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:42.340
course, we have to realize that the fact that these systems work differently,

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:44.245
is also the source of the performance.

00:19:44.245 --> 00:19:45.700
They are different to us,

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:49.870
that's why they can provide something that we don't have.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:51.835
There is a little bit of distinction.

00:19:51.835 --> 00:19:57.160
In particular, I think the big question here is,

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:00.250
how do we navigate this trade-off

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:05.335
between letting the systems extract the patterns the way they see fit,

00:20:05.335 --> 00:20:08.800
which again might lead to also new discoveries,

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:14.020
discoveries we actually want to and discoveries to have versus our instinct naturally like

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.360
a responsibility to make sure we understand what

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:20.095
the systems are doing and that they don't go out of bounds.

00:20:20.095 --> 00:20:23.110
The way I like to put it is that,

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:24.670
we have a tendency,

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:28.705
the way these things are phrased in the popular science that we think of ML as a hammer.

00:20:28.705 --> 00:20:31.990
Have a problem, you just bring ML on this and you are done.

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:33.280
It's really not a hammer.

00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:34.600
If I have to use an analogy,

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:35.830
I would call it a sharp knife.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:37.900
Something that can work very well if you know what you're

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:40.315
doing but you can hurt yourself badly.

00:20:40.315 --> 00:20:45.205
In particular, again, correlation extractor is what I call the double-edged sword of ML.

00:20:45.205 --> 00:20:49.435
It gives its power but also the problems within particular,

00:20:49.435 --> 00:20:54.220
realizing that you look at the hiring data and realize that well,

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:58.390
you can get idea that if you play Lacrosse and your first name is

00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:02.605
Jared, then you are destined to be good employee.

00:21:02.605 --> 00:21:05.290
All of these things exactly stem from

00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:08.260
mistaking correlation for causation

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:12.265
and, of course, on top of the biases that our models have.

00:21:12.265 --> 00:21:14.560
Data matters a lot, again,

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:17.050
this is at the core of the robust and safety problems,

00:21:17.050 --> 00:21:20.320
but it also drives a lot of ML-related inequities.

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:27.175
It also leads to many other unintended consequences that I will not go into.

00:21:27.175 --> 00:21:32.110
Let me conclude by saying that what I view as where we are hopefully trying to go,

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:34.135
is truly deployable MLs.

00:21:34.135 --> 00:21:37.670
ML that we can deploy and we can feel good about.

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.230
Again, this is not something that machine

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:43.570
learning researchers or even computer scientists solve.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:45.625
This is a much broader question.

00:21:45.625 --> 00:21:50.860
Yes, there is a role for machine learning researchers and engineers, but they also need to

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:53.545
recognize that these are really complex and messy tasks,

00:21:53.545 --> 00:21:56.500
and real-world data is really not what they can just nicely model.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:00.205
There is more to it. They need to understand that role.

00:22:00.205 --> 00:22:02.680
Then, there is a role for domain practitioners.

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:06.910
They really have to clarify how the data, the systems are

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:11.604
supposed to use was collected and also what are the actual correct objectives

00:22:11.604 --> 00:22:13.675
that we should be after to?

00:22:13.675 --> 00:22:17.410
Finally, and again, I don't know how interested Nisheeth will be going in that direction,

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:19.720
but I find it very interesting,

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:23.230
is you have to realize that there's a role for decision and policy-makers.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:27.475
You need to acknowledge that there are trade-offs there,

00:22:27.475 --> 00:22:31.495
that we should decide how we want to navigate them.

00:22:31.495 --> 00:22:34.540
Again, this had the society because different trade-offs might play

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:38.275
differently for different stakeholders, and we need to make a decision.

00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:41.410
I really talk too much about things that relate to my work.

00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:43.600
If you want to learn more, you can see it here.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.030
I just want to close it off at saying

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:50.200
that robustness and reliability is really important, and it's

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.290
an important question, but it's just a part of

00:22:53.290 --> 00:22:56.770
the bigger question of this phenomenon that we are seeing right now.

00:22:56.770 --> 00:23:01.420
That data-driven decision-making percolates just about every aspect of our lives.

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:04.750
At first, it was things like algorithmic trading that most of us didn't care about

00:23:04.750 --> 00:23:15.775
but then slowly but surely it started bordering and touch on really scary things.

00:23:15.775 --> 00:23:19.465
I think the two points that I want to highlight here is that first of all,

00:23:19.465 --> 00:23:21.940
we do not understand what these consequences of

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.850
using data-driven decision-making in this context have.

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:29.920
We are only discovering it almost by just trying it out and seeing what happens.

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:33.370
Really not the best way to do it, but that's where we are.

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:37.740
Again, I want to emphasize because this is very important,

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:42.630
is that in all the things I talk about in my work, especially in the vision,

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:44.670
yes, it's difficult to get

00:23:44.670 --> 00:23:46.990
reliable and robust systems and there are many fundamental reasons for that.

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:52.930
But at least we agree what robust and reliable systems mean like.

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.135
We have a gold standard of human vision and we

00:23:55.135 --> 00:23:58.195
can agree if this is a correct behavior or not.

00:23:58.195 --> 00:24:01.150
In many of the questions that we pursue in

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:04.135
this broader agenda and in particular the ones that Nisheeth touches on,

00:24:04.135 --> 00:24:08.650
we don't even agree necessarily what the desired behavior is.

00:24:08.650 --> 00:24:11.230
We can really disagree about what is fair,

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:16.450
what is not fair, and many other concepts that come out in the context of the systems.

00:24:16.450 --> 00:24:19.675
That's something that we need to recognize upfront.

00:24:19.675 --> 00:24:22.675
I really view it as a really important challenge

00:24:22.675 --> 00:24:25.990
for us scientists, but also going beyond that.

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:28.630
In particular, the questions like how we as scientists can

00:24:28.630 --> 00:24:31.735
engage with the real-world to provide some.

00:24:31.735 --> 00:24:34.270
We should not be telling people what to do but at least we

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:36.745
should be able to make sure they understand what's the problem.

00:24:36.745 --> 00:24:41.980
But anyway, I talked enough and I would like to just move to the discussion.

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:46.630
I have a few questions, but I'm happy to give it to Nisheeth based

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:52.420
on this talk, but I'm happy to also follow Priya's lead how we go next.

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:54.400
Thank you so much, Aleksander for

00:24:54.400 --> 00:25:04.585
this wonderful defining of your vantage point and your point of view on these matters.

00:25:04.585 --> 00:25:08.560
Before we go right into the question,

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:16.300
I have a couple of simple questions that come from a slightly different direction.

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:21.760
You know machine learning and AI have been considered

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:23.200
hugely promising, for example,

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:27.235
in my fields in astronomy and physics and other disciplines.

00:25:27.235 --> 00:25:30.190
As a way for revealing, as you said,

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:35.635
these underlying correlations that may or may not have causation,

00:25:35.635 --> 00:25:42.640
the challenge is that in physics at least, we have laws that structure our expectation,

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:46.705
so we know that certain quantities are conserved and so on, so forth.

00:25:46.705 --> 00:25:51.565
I would like you both to also engage a little bit about

00:25:51.565 --> 00:25:57.040
the intellectual domain and discovery space.

00:25:57.040 --> 00:26:01.855
What these methods are doing for discovery space?

00:26:01.855 --> 00:26:06.070
We're all enthusiastic, but are there things we need to be cautious about?

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:10.600
What are the things you would alert us scientists in other fields?

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:15.310
We're all really excited and want to use machine learning to do X, Y, and Z.

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:22.180
Then, I also realize that in the example that you showed us with ImageNet,

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:27.595
isn't there an issue of incompleteness of data?

00:26:27.595 --> 00:26:31.045
Isn't that a fundamental inherent limit?

00:26:31.045 --> 00:26:39.985
Not all attributes of dogliness are known, right?

00:26:39.985 --> 00:26:43.315
Hypothetically, would you both,

00:26:43.315 --> 00:26:46.600
I mean I'd like to hear both of your responses to this.

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:52.810
If hypothetically we were able to define the entire space of attributes,

00:26:52.810 --> 00:26:57.940
complex multi-dimensional space of all the attributes that define dogliness,

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.540
then would the recognition,

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:04.030
in your opinion, would that be perfect?

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:05.515
Would that be enough?

00:27:05.515 --> 00:27:14.200
How important is the incompleteness other than the planting and biases the examples?

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:19.165
Let me start by taking a stab at both of your questions, Priya.

00:27:19.165 --> 00:27:20.530
For the first question,

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:27.925
I think this is very important to take into account the invariances in the data domain.

00:27:27.925 --> 00:27:33.130
There is a branch of machine learning which is beginning to do that.

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:38.080
Even the examples that Aleksander was showing,

00:27:38.080 --> 00:27:40.060
images, they have invariances.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.430
In particular, you can rotate an image and

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:47.815
your opinion about the image should not change.

00:27:47.815 --> 00:27:49.690
As far as I remember,

00:27:49.690 --> 00:27:51.520
Aleksander, correct me if I'm wrong.

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:55.435
I mean, the original machine learning systems are not even robust to

00:27:55.435 --> 00:27:59.620
these kinds of the simple rotations. Am I right?

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:01.270
Yes. It's all right.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:08.395
I think the issue there was that if we know symmetries of the data,

00:28:08.395 --> 00:28:14.305
we have to then design algorithms that take into account those symmetries.

00:28:14.305 --> 00:28:20.275
This then leads to problems on manifolds because the moment you have symmetries,

00:28:20.275 --> 00:28:22.810
the mathematics gets complex.

00:28:22.810 --> 00:28:25.075
The traditional thing that

00:28:25.075 --> 00:28:28.195
Aleksander was describing is a straightforward Euclidean space.

00:28:28.195 --> 00:28:30.700
You're doing optimization in Euclidean space.

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:33.580
On manifolds, optimization gets complicated

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:37.240
because you only know your tangent space at this point.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:40.480
You don't necessarily know how you're going to take

00:28:40.480 --> 00:28:44.200
long steps and the computational cost starts increasing.

00:28:44.200 --> 00:28:48.295
But there is something called Geometric Deep Learning, which is coming up.

00:28:48.295 --> 00:28:50.620
To your second question,

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:53.365
I can give a more mathematical answer to that.

00:28:53.365 --> 00:28:57.010
I mean, so the question of what does dogliness mean

00:28:57.010 --> 00:29:00.100
is I'm not sure one can ever define that.

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:03.160
But in his classic,

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.830
beautiful theory on PAC learnability,

00:29:05.830 --> 00:29:10.630
Leslie Valiant who is a Turing Award winner and one of the founders of learning theory,

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:18.175
he did introduce the notion of a distribution from these data accounts.

00:29:18.175 --> 00:29:21.295
The setting of learning theory is the following,

00:29:21.295 --> 00:29:27.220
that you have some unknown distribution and you're given independent samples from

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:30.655
that distribution and so you should only

00:29:30.655 --> 00:29:37.315
be tested against samples from that distribution.

00:29:37.315 --> 00:29:41.065
The assumption, and Aleksander will talk much more about it,

00:29:41.065 --> 00:29:44.785
the assumption in many of these algorithms is that,

00:29:44.785 --> 00:29:46.660
which is wrong as you point out,

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:49.285
it's not necessarily incompleteness.

00:29:49.285 --> 00:29:52.480
It's like we don't know what dogliness is,

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:55.300
what this distribution is and we certainly don't.

00:29:55.300 --> 00:29:56.980
What is the ideal?

00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:01.165
We certainly don't believe that we have IID, the independent samples from

00:30:01.165 --> 00:30:06.445
the space of dogliness and I think Aleksander made this point by these fishes,

00:30:06.445 --> 00:30:08.990
which is very important.

00:30:11.310 --> 00:30:13.345
I mean, in this literature,

00:30:13.345 --> 00:30:20.560
there is a theory which says that if you are given samples from the true distribution,

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:23.080
then you can quantify

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:27.910
the quality of the algorithm with respect to samples from the same distribution.

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:30.370
Yeah, I guess that's all I have to say.

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:35.470
I was curious because the reason I asked with dogliness is because, obviously,

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:38.695
the more real-world complex issue is,

00:30:38.695 --> 00:30:40.825
what is the ideal candidate?

00:30:40.825 --> 00:30:44.815
When we are making candidate selections and so on.

00:30:44.815 --> 00:30:47.780
These things also keep changing with time.

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:52.345
I mean, as a society, and I'll move this discussion a little bit to what

00:30:52.345 --> 00:30:54.850
Aleksander was saying towards the end of his talk,

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:57.175
that as a society,

00:30:57.175 --> 00:30:59.440
we might have grown up with two genders,

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:03.790
male and female, but today, even that's not true.

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:06.380
It's very dynamically changing.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:09.880
I mean, forget fairness,

00:31:09.880 --> 00:31:11.650
I mean fairness with respect to what?

00:31:11.650 --> 00:31:14.125
We don't even know what that 'what' is.

00:31:14.125 --> 00:31:19.390
We can define fairness once we know what to define fairness with respect to.

00:31:19.390 --> 00:31:23.800
If the notion of dog and cat is evolving over time,

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:26.650
then how can we think about designing

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:29.470
algorithms which are shared with respect to dogs and cats?

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:31.735
I think that's even a more fundamental issue,

00:31:31.735 --> 00:31:35.200
at least when it comes to things like bias.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.245
Now, probably, this is not happening in the physical world,

00:31:38.245 --> 00:31:43.915
where the equations that govern black holes are changing.

00:31:43.915 --> 00:31:46.150
At least the assumption is,

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:50.050
that the laws of physics are not mutating over time.

00:31:50.050 --> 00:31:54.445
There are some differences between these areas.

00:31:54.445 --> 00:31:57.280
Maybe one extreme is really the physical world,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:31:58.690
where things are very nice,

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:01.435
well-defined, or at least you have some idea of the model.

00:32:01.435 --> 00:32:04.720
I would add that even if you go

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:08.020
back to the history of physics and how Kepler discovered his laws,

00:32:08.020 --> 00:32:09.790
I mean, I'm not an expert on all this stuff,

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:14.695
but looking at data, he discovered new laws.

00:32:14.695 --> 00:32:17.620
It is entirely possible that by looking at data,

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:19.810
you can revise the laws of physics.

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:23.000
I'm sure you're working on things like that.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:31.530
It is true that many of the laws were actually empirically discovered.

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:33.975
That is definitely true.

00:32:33.975 --> 00:32:36.120
When the data was not as complex either,

00:32:36.120 --> 00:32:38.345
and now the data is much more complex.

00:32:38.345 --> 00:32:41.770
But Aleksander, I might invite you to

00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:46.045
ask Nisheeth any specific questions that you had in mind.

00:32:46.045 --> 00:32:50.050
Sure, let me just complement to what Nisheeth said.

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.810
I just zoom out a little bit.

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:57.970
I think if you think about how we create knowledge around the world, they're like two.

00:32:57.970 --> 00:33:00.160
Again, at the very high level, two ways of doing it.

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:03.610
One of them is we postulate models like that's actually much of physics.

00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:05.260
There is a law of physics,

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:10.870
and we say this is how the universe works.

00:33:10.870 --> 00:33:13.675
Based on that, we derive the experiments.

00:33:13.675 --> 00:33:16.360
We have predictions of how things should work.

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:17.770
Then there's data-driven thing,

00:33:17.770 --> 00:33:20.140
when we exactly look at the observations,

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:23.530
and we try to crystallize from this.

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:28.225
What symmetry is in, what patterns we see.

00:33:28.225 --> 00:33:30.685
So clearly, machine learning is in the second count,

00:33:30.685 --> 00:33:33.220
and that's why I can imagine and I can

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:35.830
actually share with you some excitement about doing that.

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:38.170
There's a lot of, I would say,

00:33:38.170 --> 00:33:41.800
practical challenges there which already Nisheeth mentioned to.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:48.680
The sample complexity of learning something reliable from data is quite enormous.

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:54.085
The way this is called the machine learning is a prior.

00:33:54.085 --> 00:33:57.205
You had these laws of universe, you need to have a prior.

00:33:57.205 --> 00:33:58.480
You posted from symmetries.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:33:59.920
Now, even to get this in work,

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.960
you needed to have convolutional neural networks,

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:06.880
so you had to postulate this spatial invariance as a way

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:10.930
to really reduce the space of concepts that the modal explored.

00:34:10.930 --> 00:34:15.460
Similar things need to happen in this context of different sciences,

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:21.710
and yes, geometric deep learning is trying to provide a framework for that.

00:34:22.140 --> 00:34:30.250
This is one approach of understanding the world, building knowledge about the world.

00:34:30.250 --> 00:34:32.860
But on the other end, of course, the answer is no.

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:34.675
They are really very complementary.

00:34:34.675 --> 00:34:37.420
Like in some ways, I don't think that

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:41.245
ever you will learn what dog is just purely from data.

00:34:41.245 --> 00:34:43.210
But I could imagine that

00:34:43.210 --> 00:34:46.840
there is certain notion of something data-driven,

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:49.780
the texture of the fur, you can learn maybe from data.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:54.910
But then there are certain concepts that a dog has four legs actually.

00:34:54.910 --> 00:34:57.655
My concept of a dog is changing as well.

00:34:57.655 --> 00:35:00.460
Other aspects that might be encoding to modeling.

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:04.180
Again, there is a very interesting question of imaging.

00:35:04.180 --> 00:35:08.080
Aleksander, it's not so much if one method is better than the other.

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:11.080
The question is in the scientific domain,

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:16.570
it is which is more reliable in terms of explanatory power and predictive power?

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:19.030
Okay. That's great.

00:35:19.030 --> 00:35:20.890
The question is what reliable means.

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:26.409
Like clearly, what we are striving to is to have beautiful equations.

00:35:26.409 --> 00:35:34.570
That's what physics excel is like beautiful equations that explain everything.

00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:34.674
And predict.

00:35:34.674 --> 00:35:36.280
And predict, of course. You can have a beautiful algorithm that doesn't predict anything,

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:37.810
then you don't care.

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:40.450
But of course, we know that sometimes when we really want

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:43.660
to fit real-world like we looked at [inaudible] model.

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:45.430
Things become a bit messy saying,

00:35:45.430 --> 00:35:47.920
here we have these constants and so on.

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:51.445
It's amazing. It predicts so much.

00:35:51.445 --> 00:35:54.640
But yes, the reliability here is,

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:57.025
I think in the end, the way we as humans,

00:35:57.025 --> 00:36:00.730
I don't think we can communicate via data to each other what things.

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:04.510
I think in the end, we are biased towards models and priors

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:08.230
because that's the only thing that is compact enough for us to do.

00:36:08.230 --> 00:36:10.450
In some ways, again, I don't want to go to philosophical,

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:14.230
but to me, the role of machine learning is to be a bit of opposite to us.

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:19.360
I think one big power of ML is just being really able to understand,

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:21.595
infer simple rules from a lot of data.

00:36:21.595 --> 00:36:24.115
Just being able to cognitively just process the data,

00:36:24.115 --> 00:36:26.530
even if not very deep way, something complementary.

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:27.925
Like I think we can go very deep,

00:36:27.925 --> 00:36:32.725
but on a very small volume of data.

00:36:32.725 --> 00:36:35.650
ML can do a bit opposite in some ways,

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:39.895
that's why I never really like when people say about supplanting humans with ML.

00:36:39.895 --> 00:36:43.465
I think it's really should be always about complementing humans from ML.

00:36:43.465 --> 00:36:46.300
Since our notion of reliability is really we like to have a

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:50.665
concise model that predicts, the data-driven stuff

00:36:50.665 --> 00:36:52.375
always will be less reliable.

00:36:52.375 --> 00:36:55.300
Essentially, we would like to use it to then formulate

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:58.825
the laws that we actually are comfortable with.

00:36:58.825 --> 00:37:00.400
Thank you.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:08.845
Okay. Let me ask a few questions to Nisheeth based on your talk.

00:37:08.845 --> 00:37:13.240
Essentially in the talk, you already articulated,

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.790
there is a little bit, there are two components of

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:18.445
this data-driven decision-making system.

00:37:18.445 --> 00:37:19.780
It's like one of them is data,

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.855
like what are the kinds of biases in data to be learned.

00:37:22.855 --> 00:37:24.385
But the other is the objective.

00:37:24.385 --> 00:37:27.070
In the end, you are learning the data,

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:32.605
essentially the model is performing optimization to optimize something.

00:37:32.605 --> 00:37:35.890
As we know by now, both aspects matter,

00:37:35.890 --> 00:37:39.850
so what is your thinking about the relative importance of these?

00:37:39.850 --> 00:37:47.335
And if you think about improving the way these systems work moving forward,

00:37:47.335 --> 00:37:50.350
which might be easier to improve?

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:55.670
What is the hope here in terms of improvement?

00:37:55.700 --> 00:38:04.575
In terms of impact, the objective that we

00:38:04.575 --> 00:38:11.880
optimize in many of these algorithms play a much bigger role, in my opinion.

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:14.460
Because the moment you write down an objective,

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:18.030
so let's take an example of college admissions,

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:23.790
and college admissions is something that probably everybody has personal experience with.

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:27.940
When we talk about something like college admissions,

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:33.510
we historically have been using things like SAT scores,

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:37.230
to what extent do they play

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.550
a role in the decision, that's been changing and that's debatable.

00:38:41.550 --> 00:38:45.420
But let's pretend for a moment that there are colleges

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:49.680
which will just look at your SAT score and decide whether you get in or not.

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:52.470
Now, the real question here is,

00:38:52.470 --> 00:39:00.465
is what the college looking for in a student for their university,

00:39:00.465 --> 00:39:00.840
what is the college looking for at the university,

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:07.620
and why are we using SAT score and whether it is the right thing to do?

00:39:07.620 --> 00:39:10.590
Now, we're realizing that it is not the right thing to do

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:15.060
because it measures something else compared to what we are trying to measure.

00:39:15.060 --> 00:39:17.505
The moment we create a metric,

00:39:17.505 --> 00:39:23.940
a metric in this setting is a value judgment, that we value this.

00:39:23.940 --> 00:39:28.950
The moment we create a value system by writing down an objective function,

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:35.910
certain segments of society will figure out how to be

00:39:35.910 --> 00:39:43.330
good in that value system as opposed to what the original objective of the university is.

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:46.050
To answer your question in short,

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:49.290
I think this is really important.

00:39:49.290 --> 00:39:50.790
I cannot emphasize it more.

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:54.165
At universities, we talk about diversity,

00:39:54.165 --> 00:39:58.380
and every university is right now on a path to increase diversity.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:39:59.970
I would go ahead and say here that

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:03.700
most universities don't have any idea what they are doing.

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:15.075
This brings back to your point, the second last point you made,

00:40:15.075 --> 00:40:21.760
we don't know what the implications of our policy decisions on increasing diversity is.

00:40:22.220 --> 00:40:27.675
Because in the end, you will implement these diversity decisions based on some objectives.

00:40:27.675 --> 00:40:36.855
Maybe we will have a dean of diversity who is black, and what does being black mean?

00:40:36.855 --> 00:40:40.860
Is it okay if somebody who is black but they lived in a

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:44.535
completely privileged life all throughout their life?

00:40:44.535 --> 00:40:48.495
I'm not sure to what extent these discussions are even happening,

00:40:48.495 --> 00:40:54.660
and so the objective function as to how to

00:40:54.660 --> 00:41:01.455
select a candidate for a position is misaligned as you called it.

00:41:01.455 --> 00:41:06.600
I think that data component is secondary.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:10.244
Once there is an agreement that this is what we want to optimize,

00:41:10.244 --> 00:41:13.620
that's where then comes the issue of,

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:17.925
do we know everything about the data that we need to know?

00:41:17.925 --> 00:41:23.055
This question is not specific to bias.

00:41:23.055 --> 00:41:26.160
This is in your work, this is in Priya's work.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:33.225
This happens in every discipline where data-driven discovery is happening.

00:41:33.225 --> 00:41:37.350
Do we know everything about the data that we need to know?

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:42.705
What are these systems in which we can write down the assumptions on data?

00:41:42.705 --> 00:41:51.990
For example, I talked a little bit about correlations between data,

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:54.165
so I do not know what the correlations might be.

00:41:54.165 --> 00:41:58.740
Maybe I just use an adversarial model saying

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:04.020
that because I do not know what the correlation is that we quantified by saying that

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:11.580
the adversary can introduce arbitrary correlations in data,

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:15.780
and I have a measure of how many data points the adversary can touch.

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:18.900
Based on that, I will now design an algorithm,

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:21.270
based on this knowledge, this is meta knowledge.

00:42:21.270 --> 00:42:23.925
I think we are moving in this direction.

00:42:23.925 --> 00:42:26.085
We are not moving so much in the direction

00:42:26.085 --> 00:42:28.740
of trying to understand what are the objectives,

00:42:28.740 --> 00:42:32.640
and I think that cannot be something that would be done by

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:35.070
technical or mathematical people.

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:38.940
This is where the domain expertise has to come in.

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:43.440
Even inside a domain,

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:51.455
we have to have discussions which take into account various sectors in society.

00:42:51.455 --> 00:42:54.140
For example, let me give you a controversial quote here,

00:42:54.140 --> 00:42:56.420
the Nobel Prizes got announced recently.

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:56.570
I'd like to make the discussion more interesting, I guess.

00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:56.571
The Nobel Prizes got announced recently,

00:42:56.571 --> 00:43:06.375
and the Chair of the Nobel Prize Committee, Priya, correct me if I'm wrong,

00:43:06.375 --> 00:43:10.350
comes out and says that, we are not,

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:13.050
I mean they're criticized for not having other than a single

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:17.235
woman being awarded and they come out and make some, in my opinion,

00:43:17.235 --> 00:43:23.430
outrageous statements without any understanding of what the system is.

00:43:23.430 --> 00:43:28.140
Does the system evaluate women and men in the same manner?

00:43:28.140 --> 00:43:31.575
What is the system? Who decides?

00:43:31.575 --> 00:43:34.785
If you are evaluating scientific content,

00:43:34.785 --> 00:43:38.685
please do not talk like this is a godly evaluation process.

00:43:38.685 --> 00:43:41.835
These are 100 percent human evaluation processes.

00:43:41.835 --> 00:43:44.445
This has 100 percent human biases,

00:43:44.445 --> 00:43:48.450
and we all should acknowledge that to say the least,

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:52.785
and certain groups get evaluated lower in this,

00:43:52.785 --> 00:43:55.365
not because their work is any worse.

00:43:55.365 --> 00:44:00.520
It's just because how the evaluation systems are set up in society.

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:07.350
When we evaluate, we should understand what we are evaluating,

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:09.975
and the data that is coming to us,

00:44:09.975 --> 00:44:14.760
would the data for one group be biased compared to the other?

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.780
Right from the biggest achievements that we celebrate to

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:23.220
the smallest things in places like Africa,

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:27.840
we see a huge disparity in just our understanding of data.

00:44:27.840 --> 00:44:35.970
COVID-19 has shown what significant disparity is in data.

00:44:35.970 --> 00:44:39.270
I mean, it's a long answer to your question,

00:44:39.270 --> 00:44:42.555
but I think the higher-order thing is objective,

00:44:42.555 --> 00:44:44.910
and then the lower order,

00:44:44.910 --> 00:44:48.960
which I think is currently tractable and at least we're asking the right questions,

00:44:48.960 --> 00:44:53.190
and you and I are both working in this for

00:44:53.190 --> 00:44:58.200
different objectives like reliability, responsibility, and bias.

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:04.050
I think the ultimate goal is trying to understand what we can use about the data.

00:45:04.050 --> 00:45:12.850
But isn't one fundamental issue that we may not all agree even on the metrics?

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:21.045
This is what I refer to as participatory design of metrics.

00:45:21.045 --> 00:45:23.565
In the end, we have to agree,

00:45:23.565 --> 00:45:28.560
so we have to identify which group it affects,

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:32.940
and then we have to bring them onto the table and have a discussion.

00:45:32.940 --> 00:45:39.510
But ultimately, we can go closer and closer to the true thing,

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:43.695
but we will never reach the true goal.

00:45:43.695 --> 00:45:48.855
But for the ML systems to work,

00:45:48.855 --> 00:45:54.315
do you think we need to become a society that has shared values first?

00:45:54.315 --> 00:45:56.740
Is that a prerequisite?

00:45:56.780 --> 00:46:01.230
Aleksander, I will let you take this.

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:04.995
This is actually going to my next question in some way, but before that,

00:46:04.995 --> 00:46:07.260
I liked the point you made, Nisheeth.

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:09.495
Just one more thing is exactly like that,

00:46:09.495 --> 00:46:13.650
there is an interesting paper that shows that even if you try

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:18.270
to make a fairness intervention in context of consumer lending.

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:21.540
You want to make sure that certain groups which are,

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:25.905
for systemic reasons, under-privileged,

00:46:25.905 --> 00:46:30.319
that they actually do affirmative action to improve their lending to them,

00:46:30.319 --> 00:46:34.660
which if you look at this, we did something good in the world.

00:46:35.090 --> 00:46:38.130
Again, remember, we are not only predicting things,

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:40.350
we're are making decisions, and since we have consequences,

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:44.535
it turns out if you look at the long-term dynamics that comes out of that.

00:46:44.535 --> 00:46:49.380
Some of these people actually, it's really hard for them to pay back the loans.

00:46:49.380 --> 00:46:51.575
Again, there are some systemic reasons behind that,

00:46:51.575 --> 00:46:52.820
why they don't have enough wealth,

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:54.680
but this is reality.

00:46:54.680 --> 00:46:58.460
You are letting these people get a loan.

00:46:58.460 --> 00:47:00.890
They actually are more likely to not pay it

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:03.950
back and that hurts their credit score even more.

00:47:03.950 --> 00:47:07.835
In the end, by having intervention that was supposed to do the right thing,

00:47:07.835 --> 00:47:14.590
you actually hurt them even more as a population moving forward and even individuals.

00:47:15.770 --> 00:47:19.200
This is something that both Nisheeth lament.

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:23.520
There's not enough recognition of importance of the objectives.

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:26.520
In the end, this is about how I make my accuracy better,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:30.145
not even thinking of accuracy on what and what does it represent?

00:47:30.145 --> 00:47:32.340
The moment you start peeling under this,

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:34.560
you realize that there is a lot of problems,

00:47:34.560 --> 00:47:37.095
a lot of assumptions here.

00:47:37.095 --> 00:47:39.930
Now, going into my next question because exactly,

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:41.310
I think what I'm learning,

00:47:41.310 --> 00:47:46.395
my work causes me to think a lot and your questions and discussions sync.

00:47:46.395 --> 00:47:48.960
There's little bit of double standards here.

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:52.395
Like in some ways we say, "This machine learning system,

00:47:52.395 --> 00:47:55.410
it's unfair, it's doing this,

00:47:55.410 --> 00:47:58.845
doing that, and what is the exact objective that it's using?"

00:47:58.845 --> 00:48:01.125
On the other hand, we have human decision-making.

00:48:01.125 --> 00:48:03.945
First of all, much of what the machine learning does

00:48:03.945 --> 00:48:09.360
is to just uncover the biases and inequities of human decision-making,

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:11.250
just makes it apparent,

00:48:11.250 --> 00:48:14.220
maybe that's the uncomfortable truth here that we don't want to acknowledge.

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:17.835
The other thing is saying, the admissions decisions

00:48:17.835 --> 00:48:20.930
are made by human-based committee which in the past

00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:24.440
talked about character and other things and the feat,

00:48:24.440 --> 00:48:26.815
and there is no one,

00:48:26.815 --> 00:48:32.055
in some ways, we seemed to be okay with that and now we are saying,

00:48:32.055 --> 00:48:33.240
this machine learning things,

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:35.595
they are so bad, we should not be using them.

00:48:35.595 --> 00:48:38.100
I think that a little bit of reckoning here,

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:41.385
and I think that's what Priya actually talked about and saying that,

00:48:41.385 --> 00:48:46.140
yes, and I agree with Nisheeth but we should have an honest conversation about objectives,

00:48:46.140 --> 00:48:48.090
in particular, if we talk about Nobel Prizes,

00:48:48.090 --> 00:48:49.710
let's go a little bit there.

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:56.310
Why did we assume that some committee of people in Sweden decide?

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:58.845
Why are we even giving away the Nobel Prizes?

00:48:58.845 --> 00:49:01.005
This is still arbitrary.

00:49:01.005 --> 00:49:02.580
There could be some value in this,

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:05.760
but the point is it seems to be a proxy for like

00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:08.130
if you've got a Nobel Prize, you are a God of science.

00:49:08.130 --> 00:49:09.705
If you didn't, you didn't.

00:49:09.705 --> 00:49:12.990
There's marketing associated to it.

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:14.775
Yes, the point is that,

00:49:14.775 --> 00:49:19.020
I'm not saying every award has a problem, which is fine.

00:49:19.020 --> 00:49:20.850
As long as you have an honest conversation that

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:23.290
this is what these things are

00:49:23.330 --> 00:49:28.000
and we should have an honest like it's a recognition of that.

00:49:28.160 --> 00:49:31.125
I feel compelled to point out,

00:49:31.125 --> 00:49:35.235
one of the things that this spokesperson was completely,

00:49:35.235 --> 00:49:39.375
I think he revealed his ignorance in so many ways.

00:49:39.375 --> 00:49:42.060
I mean, the basic point is that there is

00:49:42.060 --> 00:49:47.395
a fundamental difference in the number of women who do get nominated in the first place,

00:49:47.395 --> 00:49:51.470
let alone whether they are considered or not and eventually get the prize.

00:49:51.470 --> 00:50:00.420
To not even understand and acknowledge that that is a systemic bottleneck,

00:50:01.280 --> 00:50:06.165
it was quite depressing and irritating.

00:50:06.165 --> 00:50:08.655
Yeah. But I think, Aleksander, you're right.

00:50:08.655 --> 00:50:11.130
This is how I think about this.

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:15.690
I realized that this is why this area got so much importance because

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:19.770
in the end, it definitely proved that humans are biased.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:22.680
Because it's not like we're receiving these algorithms

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:25.905
from other civilizations, we are constructing these.

00:50:25.905 --> 00:50:27.525
We are collecting the data.

00:50:27.525 --> 00:50:28.740
Whatever is the reason,

00:50:28.740 --> 00:50:31.740
it's a definitive proof that we are biased.

00:50:31.740 --> 00:50:36.600
I think that's why things like Kathy O'Neill's book that came out,

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:39.460
it caught media attention.

00:50:41.900 --> 00:50:46.570
I think it is giving us insight.

00:50:46.670 --> 00:50:52.710
It's providing a lens to our own society, to our own self.

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:55.800
It is. But if you look at the coverage of this, it is not

00:50:55.800 --> 00:50:58.800
that humans are biased, it's algorithmic bias.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:01.365
We are really protecting ourselves from

00:51:01.365 --> 00:51:05.010
letting this implication sink in. This is interesting.

00:51:05.010 --> 00:51:09.525
Again, even if you think about people who talk about these things,

00:51:09.525 --> 00:51:12.300
even they don't make this point strongly enough because

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:15.210
maybe it should be uncomfortable truth and so on.

00:51:15.210 --> 00:51:18.465
Again, I agree. This is actually definitive proof.

00:51:18.465 --> 00:51:23.175
I would say that while I was thinking about this talk, I realized this.

00:51:23.175 --> 00:51:28.590
I mean, I would be a little bit gentler because it is an obvious thing in retrospect,

00:51:28.590 --> 00:51:35.385
but it took me also a few years to just have this realization that this is.

00:51:35.385 --> 00:51:37.485
But maybe there are people.

00:51:37.485 --> 00:51:39.900
Certainly, a very valid point

00:51:39.900 --> 00:51:43.065
now that you mentioned and that people say algorithms are biased.

00:51:43.065 --> 00:51:46.150
They don't say that human-designed algorithms are biased.

00:51:46.150 --> 00:51:52.835
As if algorithms are being shipped to us from MIT, which is another planet.

00:51:52.835 --> 00:51:56.670
Only good things come from MIT.

00:51:58.840 --> 00:52:01.560
It's almost like a rock shock test.

00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:04.275
It's like saying, you are designing an algorithm and saying,

00:52:04.275 --> 00:52:05.670
what should be the objective?

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:07.515
Then you run it through the data.

00:52:07.515 --> 00:52:12.930
Only then you realize, that's not what I meant or maybe that's what I meant all the time,

00:52:12.930 --> 00:52:18.250
but now it's actually laid bare to us.

00:52:20.630 --> 00:52:24.540
This is great. That's a very important realization.

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:29.325
It hits you a bit hard once you make it because you realize, that's it.

00:52:29.325 --> 00:52:32.475
Anyway, let's not go too much into that.

00:52:32.475 --> 00:52:36.390
But let's try to see a little bit of maybe positive spin to all of this.

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:42.390
Again, clearly using blindly data-driven decision-making is really bad idea.

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:44.880
I think hopefully we all got that message well.

00:52:44.880 --> 00:52:46.260
Not all got that message,

00:52:46.260 --> 00:52:48.300
but hopefully, we are getting this message.

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:49.590
But now let's think about like,

00:52:49.590 --> 00:52:55.950
what do we think is the correct or a bit more beneficial and inclusive?

00:52:55.950 --> 00:53:00.885
I'm talking about shared prosperity beneficial way of using such systems.

00:53:00.885 --> 00:53:03.660
What are the places where we should be using them?

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:06.915
How should we be using them? Do you have any suggestions here, Nisheeth?

00:53:06.915 --> 00:53:10.545
It takes me back to the previous point, for us,

00:53:10.545 --> 00:53:14.400
we need to as a society first have some shared values.

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:18.870
We need to first agree on what are the values.

00:53:18.870 --> 00:53:22.980
There are contexts in which you can imagine that there are shared values.

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:26.160
Let's say if you're an organization, a university,

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:28.860
then in such small university,

00:53:28.860 --> 00:53:31.815
as you can imagine, that people have some shared values,

00:53:31.815 --> 00:53:35.580
and there I think we have to work together to figure

00:53:35.580 --> 00:53:40.230
out the right objectives that we are optimizing,

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:44.415
making sure that we know everything about the data,

00:53:44.415 --> 00:53:50.610
and that we can know and then design policies or

00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:58.620
algorithms based on such inclusive and parallel thinking.

00:53:58.620 --> 00:54:03.420
I'm talking more in general terms of algorithms in which I include policies,

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:04.815
and not just machine learning,

00:54:04.815 --> 00:54:08.325
just to be very clear in this context.

00:54:08.325 --> 00:54:16.335
I should mention that the machine learning Aleksander does

00:54:16.335 --> 00:54:26.325
is the state of the art and he's using some really deep architectures to learn things,

00:54:26.325 --> 00:54:29.685
and the world of fairness and bias

00:54:29.685 --> 00:54:36.330
has not reached that point that it can start grappling with objects like this.

00:54:36.330 --> 00:54:46.005
The reason is actually fairly simple on this point because it's mathematical.

00:54:46.005 --> 00:54:48.465
Let me just explain.

00:54:48.465 --> 00:54:51.990
I think this is important for some people,

00:54:51.990 --> 00:54:55.860
Aleksander would probably explain it better, but let me just try.

00:54:55.860 --> 00:54:58.860
When we talk about objective functions,

00:54:58.860 --> 00:55:03.705
there is some assumption that when we will now solve this optimization problem,

00:55:03.705 --> 00:55:07.240
there is this one point that we're trying to reach.

00:55:08.660 --> 00:55:16.230
But there is no such thing in many of these models that Aleksander studies.

00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:19.515
They have very, very complex landscapes.

00:55:19.515 --> 00:55:23.175
They have many, many local optima.

00:55:23.175 --> 00:55:28.335
What shall I do as a person who cares about fairness?

00:55:28.335 --> 00:55:31.815
Different points on this landscape,

00:55:31.815 --> 00:55:38.070
there may be completely different properties related to fairness.

00:55:38.070 --> 00:55:40.890
From an accuracy of performance point of view,

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:43.480
you can select maybe one.

00:55:46.220 --> 00:55:52.455
The thing works in some way because all of these local optimal points are good points.

00:55:52.455 --> 00:55:53.940
But from the point of view of fairness,

00:55:53.940 --> 00:55:58.440
it's not at all clear how to even do that. What do you think?

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.155
Nisheeth, just to chime in quickly,

00:56:01.155 --> 00:56:06.990
but these multiple optima, maxima or whatever in that landscape,

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:11.130
don't they represent basically value trade-offs?

00:56:11.130 --> 00:56:16.155
You are trading something, there is no one clear-cut solution,

00:56:16.155 --> 00:56:20.205
but that the solution space represents,

00:56:20.205 --> 00:56:27.825
in the case of these more social domain problems like trade-offs.

00:56:27.825 --> 00:56:32.475
But I think what we're talking about are some really, at least,

00:56:32.475 --> 00:56:37.695
I view them as very ugly landscapes, where it's not I think

00:56:37.695 --> 00:56:44.775
what we're imagining that there's multiple optimum solution,

00:56:44.775 --> 00:56:46.410
they are related by some symmetry,

00:56:46.410 --> 00:56:47.790
and so there's some trade-off.

00:56:47.790 --> 00:56:52.470
I don't think anybody understands this. I mean, Aleksander.

00:56:52.470 --> 00:56:54.030
Complicated.

00:56:54.030 --> 00:56:56.460
Definitely, no one understands it.

00:56:56.460 --> 00:56:59.700
Again, I think I liked the point you made earlier that,

00:56:59.700 --> 00:57:01.695
you made in the context of data,

00:57:01.695 --> 00:57:04.335
we don't even know what we want to accomplish.

00:57:04.335 --> 00:57:08.280
Figuring out how to make this model do what you want to accomplish,

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:10.125
that's almost a secondary concern,

00:57:10.125 --> 00:57:12.660
even though that's not being nontrivial.

00:57:12.660 --> 00:57:19.425
Again, there's just so much not thinking about what we're even trying to do.

00:57:19.425 --> 00:57:22.530
We have problems. We have been enforcing the behavior we want.

00:57:22.530 --> 00:57:24.960
But I wouldn't say this is the root of the product.

00:57:24.960 --> 00:57:26.550
This is the most important challenge right now,

00:57:26.550 --> 00:57:28.875
even though of course, we should figure out.

00:57:28.875 --> 00:57:32.190
This is mainly what you are working in the context of fairness.

00:57:32.190 --> 00:57:34.995
That's something that also I do in the context of reliability saying,

00:57:34.995 --> 00:57:37.395
maybe even like what robust means,

00:57:37.395 --> 00:57:38.940
it may be hard to define.

00:57:38.940 --> 00:57:42.795
But however, they provide you with tools that if you think you know what it is,

00:57:42.795 --> 00:57:47.835
I can help you to make your model satisfy what you are looking for.

00:57:47.835 --> 00:57:50.595
But we need to recognize that this is a challenge,

00:57:50.595 --> 00:57:54.720
but it's secondary to me in terms of what the really important question is.

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:58.050
There's a very interesting audience question that I

00:57:58.050 --> 00:58:01.575
would like to now pull up and articulate.

00:58:01.575 --> 00:58:07.545
It's from Ferdous, who was one of our usual gang attendees.

00:58:07.545 --> 00:58:10.695
He's asking, can you comment on the implications of

00:58:10.695 --> 00:58:14.460
AI/ML as applied in the area of security intelligence,

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:18.130
facial recognition, surveillance, and others?

00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:21.055
Aleksander, go first.

00:58:21.055 --> 00:58:25.430
Sorry, because I needed to tell someone that because we're going

00:58:25.430 --> 00:58:31.595
a bit overtime. Could you repeat? I'm sorry.

00:58:31.595 --> 00:58:36.305
It's AI/ML applied in the area of security and intelligence.

00:58:36.305 --> 00:58:39.620
The facial recognition, surveillance, and so on.

00:58:39.620 --> 00:58:44.195
Yes. I think this is scary.

00:58:44.195 --> 00:58:46.835
To be honest, when I work on my stuff,

00:58:46.835 --> 00:58:51.185
there are some moments in the night where you are thinking,

00:58:51.185 --> 00:58:53.525
"What are we enabling?"

00:58:53.525 --> 00:58:56.930
Even though, of course, the uses we are using our tools for

00:58:56.930 --> 00:59:00.560
I would say are I feel pretty confident and more about them.

00:59:00.560 --> 00:59:06.300
There are clearly all the ways in which these things can be misused.

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:11.465
Going back to our discussion earlier is,

00:59:11.465 --> 00:59:15.860
I think what we don't really appreciate is the ability of

00:59:15.860 --> 00:59:22.250
the vastness of data that essentially whose analysis machine learning can automate.

00:59:22.250 --> 00:59:26.240
I don't think we even understand machine learning in particular or facial recognition.

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:28.565
We don't even think about,

00:59:28.565 --> 00:59:34.460
what is the level of what is enabled by doing that?

00:59:34.460 --> 00:59:36.260
To be honest, I don't know that either.

00:59:36.260 --> 00:59:37.805
If you wanted to see,

00:59:37.805 --> 00:59:39.515
of course, there's facial recognition.

00:59:39.515 --> 00:59:41.960
If we look at China and other countries,

00:59:41.960 --> 00:59:45.900
then there are some challenges there.

00:59:47.380 --> 00:59:50.435
Think about social media, for instance.

00:59:50.435 --> 00:59:53.630
If you think about the level of surveillance that's happening there,

00:59:53.630 --> 00:59:56.435
where not only what you like or not like,

00:59:56.435 --> 01:00:00.035
or what you write is taken care of, they look at your cursor.

01:00:00.035 --> 01:00:02.510
What do you even scroll over?

01:00:02.510 --> 01:00:05.780
Where your cursor is? How long does it change? All of this is ingested.

01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:09.395
It's just so easy to pull all these data,

01:00:09.395 --> 01:00:10.805
shove into the systems.

01:00:10.805 --> 01:00:13.955
Honestly, we no longer understand what you were extracting or not,

01:00:13.955 --> 01:00:16.910
but the system just works on that.

01:00:16.910 --> 01:00:20.510
To me, again, all the security aspects are very important.

01:00:20.510 --> 01:00:23.190
This is another can of worms.

01:00:23.410 --> 01:00:31.010
Not only it's about can someone compromise a system?

01:00:31.010 --> 01:00:34.600
But the question is, people think about also in terms of offensive capabilities.

01:00:34.600 --> 01:00:38.020
Can I automate my tools using ML,

01:00:38.020 --> 01:00:41.410
making them media-driven, essentially remove the slow human from the loop?

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:42.940
This is scary and there are

01:00:42.940 --> 01:00:49.080
some case scenarios that can emerge from these dynamics.

01:00:49.080 --> 01:00:52.190
Preview of this was the stock market and

01:00:52.190 --> 01:00:55.055
when you have automated trading and there was a flash crash.

01:00:55.055 --> 01:00:57.230
I think that's just an example.

01:00:57.230 --> 01:01:00.755
Again, everyone during that game wanted to get the right thing.

01:01:00.755 --> 01:01:02.150
They just wanted to get efficiency.

01:01:02.150 --> 01:01:06.960
They wanted to get this extra tick.

01:01:08.140 --> 01:01:14.990
This is such a level of the social scale of data and interactions that we don't see.

01:01:14.990 --> 01:01:17.150
But, again, let's not even talk about that.

01:01:17.150 --> 01:01:21.485
Think about social media and about what emerges from, again, simple,

01:01:21.485 --> 01:01:24.530
and I will even say simplistic objectives,

01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:28.475
and then just shoving in all this data into it.

01:01:28.475 --> 01:01:30.350
Just because it's so easy.

01:01:30.350 --> 01:01:33.995
This worries me a lot. I think that's why we should talk about privacy more.

01:01:33.995 --> 01:01:37.070
Not only because, again, privacy is, at least to me,

01:01:37.070 --> 01:01:40.820
a fundamental right but also about this might be a way to curb

01:01:40.820 --> 01:01:46.410
a little bit the unintended consequences of use of our data for optimizing everything.

01:01:47.980 --> 01:01:51.920
Nisheeth, did you want to weigh in or?

01:01:51.920 --> 01:01:57.080
I think Aleksander covered pretty much all that I would have said.

01:01:57.080 --> 01:01:59.555
I think this technology is not ready.

01:01:59.555 --> 01:02:01.490
I'll just add one thing to it.

01:02:01.490 --> 01:02:09.210
Even though you might develop a piece of algorithm in a very controlled environment,

01:02:09.700 --> 01:02:15.395
to give an analogy to cars or any other thing that people have more familiarity with.

01:02:15.395 --> 01:02:18.020
Car production was systematized.

01:02:18.020 --> 01:02:22.145
There were protocols that you have to follow and so on.

01:02:22.145 --> 01:02:26.060
It doesn't work like that with algorithms.

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:30.875
You can design an algorithm in a very safe environment with lots of guarantees,

01:02:30.875 --> 01:02:33.470
but now somebody takes that algorithm.

01:02:33.470 --> 01:02:37.115
Tinkering a car is not so easy.

01:02:37.115 --> 01:02:39.920
Common people cannot. Here you can open

01:02:39.920 --> 01:02:43.490
the Python code and just start changing the rates somewhere.

01:02:43.490 --> 01:02:48.125
Or just feed it on a few more examples.

01:02:48.125 --> 01:02:50.840
In the end, the model that you train,

01:02:50.840 --> 01:02:53.600
it can be used as starting point for something else.

01:02:53.600 --> 01:02:57.800
There are some very complex things like data poisoning and this and that.

01:02:57.800 --> 01:03:00.780
It's just a jungle out there.

01:03:05.040 --> 01:03:13.225
Even if you develop a piece of algorithm in a very precise and licensed context,

01:03:13.225 --> 01:03:15.370
when we give it to somebody to use,

01:03:15.370 --> 01:03:17.020
we basically lock it out somehow,

01:03:17.020 --> 01:03:19.820
so they can't change any aspect of it.

01:03:21.880 --> 01:03:24.020
I think that's what I would say.

01:03:24.020 --> 01:03:28.470
The technology is not ready for any of these tasks.

01:03:28.810 --> 01:03:33.365
The biases are already in there.

01:03:33.365 --> 01:03:38.570
The biases with respect to facial recognition and so on are already in there.

01:03:38.570 --> 01:03:45.005
We need to rethink how we want to design this technology completely,

01:03:45.005 --> 01:03:48.620
both from a reliability point of view and from a fairness point of view.

01:03:48.620 --> 01:03:51.920
Let's close with one final question from our audience,

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:56.810
which is related to the topics you both touched upon right now.

01:03:56.810 --> 01:04:00.350
This is also from Jenny, who's one of our regulars.

01:04:00.350 --> 01:04:03.560
How far does the sample size of data

01:04:03.560 --> 01:04:07.085
also play a role in the development of fair algorithms?

01:04:07.085 --> 01:04:09.860
Some questions can only be based on the law of

01:04:09.860 --> 01:04:13.355
small numbers instead of in the limit of large numbers.

01:04:13.355 --> 01:04:18.270
How to take sparse correlated samples into account?

01:04:22.390 --> 01:04:25.740
This is a great question.

01:04:26.200 --> 01:04:35.915
We have some works which try to take into account correlations,

01:04:35.915 --> 01:04:39.140
whether they're stochastic or adversarial,

01:04:39.140 --> 01:04:46.700
and then work out the sample complexity bounds for learning classifiers.

01:04:46.700 --> 01:04:50.540
Classification is one of the basic machine learning tasks.

01:04:50.540 --> 01:04:54.260
There's no answer for this in general.

01:04:54.260 --> 01:04:57.185
It can only be with respect to a particular problem.

01:04:57.185 --> 01:05:00.090
The problem is classification.

01:05:02.980 --> 01:05:05.810
We are working towards it,

01:05:05.810 --> 01:05:10.775
and there are certain precise mathematical solutions to these problems.

01:05:10.775 --> 01:05:16.380
I'm not sure that I can explain more without going into mathematical details.

01:05:16.690 --> 01:05:19.400
Yes. I just want to say,

01:05:19.400 --> 01:05:23.660
I know probably it wasn't meant by the question,

01:05:23.660 --> 01:05:28.340
but we need to be careful with the mantra of more data will solve problems because,

01:05:28.340 --> 01:05:31.310
again, more data might have just reinforced the same bias.

01:05:31.310 --> 01:05:35.060
We might be thinking we're making progress where we aren't.

01:05:35.060 --> 01:05:38.000
There's a lot of question about exactly

01:05:38.000 --> 01:05:42.380
data is independent or is in some ways provides different perspective.

01:05:42.380 --> 01:05:45.410
It actually would be fine if these perspectives are biased,

01:05:45.410 --> 01:05:47.480
as long as they are biased in different ways.

01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:50.450
Actually, we even have a word that tries to exactly use

01:05:50.450 --> 01:05:54.365
that perspective of I have automated this biased in some way,

01:05:54.365 --> 01:05:55.940
but these are different biases.

01:05:55.940 --> 01:06:00.440
You can play them against each other to realize what is the less biased perspective.

01:06:00.440 --> 01:06:04.925
But yes, this is a bit of a problem in the community was that people just said,

01:06:04.925 --> 01:06:08.480
"I train it on such a big dataset,

01:06:08.480 --> 01:06:09.980
so it has to be good because,

01:06:09.980 --> 01:06:11.255
again, accuracy goes up."

01:06:11.255 --> 01:06:16.205
In particular, GPT-3 is trained on a lot of texts.

01:06:16.205 --> 01:06:19.410
Again, it's very impressive.

01:06:20.170 --> 01:06:22.835
Actually, I would bet that there are a lot of

01:06:22.835 --> 01:06:26.510
biases in it that we probably wouldn't like to have.

01:06:26.510 --> 01:06:29.015
Data is definitely needed,

01:06:29.015 --> 01:06:34.650
but it's definitely not sufficient to solve this problem.

01:06:34.960 --> 01:06:42.830
Great. I think much as I would have loved us to keep talking,

01:06:42.830 --> 01:06:44.945
because it's absolutely fascinating,

01:06:44.945 --> 01:06:46.880
and learned a lot,

01:06:46.880 --> 01:06:50.120
and our audience is really thrilled to have this opportunity,

01:06:50.120 --> 01:06:52.970
I think we need to call it to a close.

01:06:52.970 --> 01:06:55.100
I would like to thank both of you,

01:06:55.100 --> 01:06:56.300
Aleksander and Nisheeth,

01:06:56.300 --> 01:07:02.000
for participating in this exciting series,

01:07:02.000 --> 01:07:06.050
trying to understand the role of data, computation,

01:07:06.050 --> 01:07:13.445
machine learning in what we can infer about the world and how we infer about the world.

01:07:13.445 --> 01:07:17.240
Thank you everyone for attending and I look

01:07:17.240 --> 01:07:21.545
forward to seeing you at the next talk in this series,

01:07:21.545 --> 01:07:23.615
which will be led by Kyle Cranmer,

01:07:23.615 --> 01:07:26.810
who is a physicist at NYU who works on machine learning

